# Images

An image should help identify, locate or understand something that text alone would not explain as well.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## When to use it

Use an informative room diagram or device photograph when it helps the household recognise the right item. Keep the name, current state and controls as readable text outside the image.

## When not to use it

Do not decorate every settings page or put essential text only inside a picture. A photograph must not become the sole evidence that a device is connected or safe to use.

## Home Assistant adaptation

The example floor plan is an original schematic, not a picture of a real home. A real installation needs its own accurate room labels and text alternative. Retain an ordinary room list as an alternative to a spatial plan, and respect access permissions for cameras and private images.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Give informative images an alt text that conveys their useful meaning. Use an empty alt for a genuinely decorative image next to equivalent text. Captions explain what to conclude, rather than repeating the alternative. Give complex diagrams an adjacent longer description.

